,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"abhin4v","user_is_author":false,"tags":["haskell"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ybk8hr","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ybk8hr/incomplete_guide_lazy_evaluation"},{"short_id":"67ygvv","created_at":"2026-09-04T10:52:56.023-05:00","title":"The new Go JSON API: twice as fast, or 1.5x slower?","url":"https://lemire.me/blog/2026/08/29/the-new-go-json-api-twice-as-fast-or-1-5x-slower/","score":3,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"weberc2","user_is_author":false,"tags":["go","performance"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/67ygvv","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/67ygvv/new_go_json_api_twice_as_fast_1_5x_slower"},{"short_id":"htrg6g","created_at":"2026-09-03T16:44:17.849-05:00","title":"An image only some users can see","url":"https://d.erenrich.net/apple.avif","score":7,"flags":1,"comment_count":7,"description":"\u003cp\u003eTry opening in different browser FF v Chrome. Or phone v desktop. Same image file but only sometimes renders.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"Try opening in different browser FF v Chrome. Or phone v desktop. Same image file but only sometimes renders.","submitter_user":"derenrich","user_is_author":true,"tags":["browsers","show"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/htrg6g","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/htrg6g/image_only_some_users_can_see"},{"short_id":"if60bl","created_at":"2026-09-03T09:03:31.735-05:00","title":"You Don't Need Initial-Scale In Your HTML","url":"https://vale.rocks/micros/20260902-1350","score":41,"flags":0,"comment_count":7,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"MatheusRich","user_is_author":false,"tags":["web"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/if60bl","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/if60bl/you_don_t_need_initial_scale_your_html"},{"short_id":"rlswbt","created_at":"2026-09-03T16:40:36.619-05:00","title":"NES dumping over audio","url":"https://mathstodon.xyz/@JordiGH/117209252363385093","score":13,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"JordiGH","user_is_author":true,"tags":["games","retrocomputing"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/rlswbt","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/rlswbt/nes_dumping_over_audio"},{"short_id":"krtqbk","created_at":"2026-09-04T08:29:06.505-05:00","title":"A defense of object-orientated programming","url":"https://codeandcake.dev/posts/2025-05-28-a-defense-of-oop","score":2,"flags":0,"comment_count":14,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"lim","user_is_author":false,"tags":["plt"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/krtqbk","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/krtqbk/defense_object_orientated_programming"},{"short_id":"nr3ucr","created_at":"2026-09-03T21:11:32.522-05:00","title":"WebDev intro course: what small changes towards progressive enhancement?","url":"","score":8,"flags":0,"comment_count":6,"description":"\u003cp\u003eFor $REASONS, I ended up in control (probably temporarily) of a rather introductory «Web Development» course with a bit of HTML/CSS/JS/Docker that I plan (and promised) to keep in a mostly recognisable shape.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI do want to add more reminders that progressive enhancement is good, though; and I ask for advice on good points/references.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003chr\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI am going to keep NodeJS backend assumption, so any JavaScript-the-language feature can be demonstrated on backend. So just learning to use JS in general is not as much of an excuse for silly things in-browser.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMy plan includes telling more than before about (and probably giving links to):\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://madcampos.dev/blog/2026/07/accessibility-from-scratch/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://madcampos.dev/blog/2026/07/accessibility-from-scratch/\u003c/a\u003e — usability and accessibility benefits of proper HTML\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://shkspr.mobi/blog/2021/01/the-unreasonable-effectiveness-of-simple-html/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://shkspr.mobi/blog/2021/01/the-unreasonable-effectiveness-of-simple-html/\u003c/a\u003e — accessibility in wider sense of things that work on pure and minimal HTML\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/defaultjavascriptjitsetting\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/defaultjavascriptjitsetting\u003c/a\u003e — MS Edge having a secure mode where JS JIT is off (and JS is slow)\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://developer.chrome.com/blog/timer-throttling-in-chrome-88/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://developer.chrome.com/blog/timer-throttling-in-chrome-88/\u003c/a\u003e — even Google admits that JS is bad for battery life…\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html\u003c/a\u003e — as a reminder not to trust too much work done on frontend\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/lwthiker/curl-impersonate\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://github.com/lwthiker/curl-impersonate\u003c/a\u003e — for general trolling (more seriously, to remind how many sites \u003cem\u003epretend\u003c/em\u003e to need JS but give Blink-based browsers fully sufficient HTML, then try to make it hard for any other browsers, and often fail)\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd I guess general stressing that document, form, and application are three different things.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI would be grateful for suggestions of other/same-but-well-made points towards progressive enhancement, not spending extra effort to break accessibility, etc. Ideally I hope to cover what has normally been covered previously, but with a viewpoint shift towards healthy approaches. Active improvements to accessibility are probably beyond what would fit and beyond what I can explain well.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"For $REASONS, I ended up in control (probably temporarily) of a rather introductory «Web Development» course with a bit of HTML/CSS/JS/Docker that I plan (and promised) to keep in a mostly recognisable shape.\r\n\r\nI do want to add more reminders that progressive enhancement is good, though; and I ask for advice on good points/references.\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nI am going to keep NodeJS backend assumption, so any JavaScript-the-language feature can be demonstrated on backend. So just learning to use JS in general is not as much of an excuse for silly things in-browser.\r\n\r\nMy plan includes telling more than before about (and probably giving links to):\r\n* https://madcampos.dev/blog/2026/07/accessibility-from-scratch/ — usability and accessibility benefits of proper HTML\r\n* https://shkspr.mobi/blog/2021/01/the-unreasonable-effectiveness-of-simple-html/ — accessibility in wider sense of things that work on pure and minimal HTML\r\n* https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/defaultjavascriptjitsetting — MS Edge having a secure mode where JS JIT is off (and JS is slow)\r\n* https://developer.chrome.com/blog/timer-throttling-in-chrome-88/ — even Google admits that JS is bad for battery life…\r\n* https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html — as a reminder not to trust too much work done on frontend\r\n* https://github.com/lwthiker/curl-impersonate — for general trolling (more seriously, to remind how many sites _pretend_ to need JS but give Blink-based browsers fully sufficient HTML, then try to make it hard for any other browsers, and often fail)\r\n\r\nAnd I guess general stressing that document, form, and application are three different things.\r\n\r\nI would be grateful for suggestions of other/same-but-well-made points towards progressive enhancement, not spending extra effort to break accessibility, etc. Ideally I hope to cover what has normally been covered previously, but with a viewpoint shift towards healthy approaches. Active improvements to accessibility are probably beyond what would fit and beyond what I can explain well.","submitter_user":"k749gtnc9l3w","user_is_author":true,"tags":["ask","web"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/nr3ucr","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/nr3ucr/webdev_intro_course_what_small_changes"},{"short_id":"asjmva","created_at":"2026-09-04T08:01:53.607-05:00","title":"Inserting State Transitions in Postgres","url":"https://thoughtbot.com/blog/inserting-state-transitions-in-postgres","score":4,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"danlamanna","user_is_author":false,"tags":["databases"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/asjmva","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/asjmva/inserting_state_transitions_postgres"}]